  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Species Sub-page · SPC·02n</p>
    <h1>Australian Raven Nesting: Breeding Season and Nest Sites</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Australian Raven (<em>Corvus coronoides</em>) builds a large stick nest in the fork of a tall eucalyptus or similar tree. Clutch size is typically 4–6 eggs. Breeding season runs approximately July–October (Southern Hemisphere late-winter to spring), with egg-laying peaking in August–September.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Sites</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Australian Raven is a tree-nester throughout its range in south-eastern and south-western Australia:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Primary sites:</strong> the fork or a stout horizontal branch of a tall tree — most commonly a large eucalyptus (gum tree) in open woodland, pastoral land, roadside trees, or parks on the edge of towns and cities.</li>
      <li><strong>Height:</strong> nests are usually placed high — typically 10–25 m above ground — giving a clear view of the surroundings and deterring ground predators.</li>
      <li><strong>Human structures:</strong> occasionally uses electricity pylons or similar structures in treeless agricultural areas.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Pairs maintain long-term territories and show site fidelity, often returning to the same tree and rebuilding or refurbishing an existing nest in successive breeding seasons.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Structure and Eggs</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The nest is a large, bulky platform of sticks and branches, lined with bark, wool, grass, and other soft materials. Completed nests measure roughly 50–80 cm in diameter. Clutch size: typically 4–6 eggs. Eggs are pale blue-green to greenish, heavily blotched with brown, grey, and olive. Incubation: approximately 20 days, mainly by the female while the male provisions her. Chicks fledge at approximately 5–6 weeks; family groups remain loosely associated for several months post-fledging.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Breeding Season</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Breeding in the Southern Hemisphere corresponds to late winter and spring:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nest building / territory reinforcement:</strong> June–July.</li>
      <li><strong>Egg-laying:</strong> July–September (peak August–September).</li>
      <li><strong>Fledging:</strong> October–November.</li>
      <li><strong>Post-fledging dependence:</strong> October–January, overlapping with early summer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Breeding pairs are strongly territorial and exclude conspecifics from the nest area throughout the season.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/species/australian-raven/">Australian Raven — species hub</a></li>
